Previously, we reported the synthesis of metal-DNA hybrid nanoparticles through coordination-mediated assembly of DNA molecules and metal ions [38]. Thus motivated, here we develop an extremely simple approach for the efficient and controlled loading of DNA molecules on nMOFs in the self-assembly of metal-DNA coordination polymers on nMOFs (termed MOF@MD, Fig. 1(a)). In comparison with the methods mentioned above, our approach allows for ultrahigh DNA loading content on nMOFs with precisely adjustable ratios via tuning of the metal-DNA shell thickness. Furthermore, by employing porphyrinic nMOFs (PCN-224) as the core, and Bcl-2 antisense ASO [39–42] and small molecular drug (doxorubicin (Dox)) as assembled shell, core–shell heterostructures (named MOF@MD-D) were prepared to achieve combinational tumor treatment (Fig. 1(b)). The porphyrinic nMOF core of MOF@MD-D could generate large quantity of reactive oxygen species (ROS) upon light irradiation, which allows to boost the endosomal escape of the system for an enhanced gene silencing efficacy. Ultimately, the hierarchical nanostructures render augmented anticancer capacity through the combination of highly efficient gene therapy and photodynamic therapy (PDT) with chemotherapy.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of PCN-224
The PCN-224 nMOFs were synthesized according to the previous method [43]. Briefly, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}\) \((20\;\mathrm{mg})\) , \(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(60\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and benzoic acid \((560\;\mathrm{mg})\) were dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) \((20\,\mathrm{\mL})\) . After sonication for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) , the mixture was heated at \(90\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(5\,\mathrm{{h}}\) with stirring. The as-synthesized PCN-224 were obtained by centrifugation \((13,000\ \mathrm{rpm},\,20\ \mathrm{min})\) . Then the products were washed 3 times with fresh DMF and re-dispersed in \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) DMF for future use.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MOF@MD
The nMOFs were washed 5 times with Millipore water and redispersed in Millipore water. In a typical synthesis of \(\mathrm{MOF@MD}\) , \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((20~\mathrm{\mM})\) was added to a centrifuge tube containing \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ASO \((1\,\mathrm{\mM})\) , \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) nMOFs and \(371~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) Millipore water. The mixture was heated at \(95~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\;\mathrm{{h}}\) after a vigorous vortex. Then the resulting \(\mathrm{MOF@MD}\) were obtained by centrifugation \((13,000\;\mathrm{rpm},\,10\;\mathrm{min})\) and re-dispersed in Millipore water. To obtain the \(\mathrm{MOF@MD}\) with different shell thicknesses, the \(\mathrm{ASO/Fe^{II}}\) molar ratio was set to the same, and the amount of ASO was varied: 1# (5 nmol ASO), 2# (10 nmol ASO), 3# (20 nmol ASO). To evaluate the ASO-loading efficiency of \(\mathrm{MOF@MD}\) , the supernatant and washing solution of the system were collected. The unloaded ASO can be determined by ultraviolet–visible (UV–Vis) spectrometry according to the extinction coefficients.
## 2.3 Quantification of DNA strands on nMOF
The zirconium content of the \(\mathrm{MOF@MDs}\) was determined by inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) on a NexION 300X instrument (Perkinelmer). Then, the particle concentration of nMOF was calculated based on the previously reported method [43]. After assembly of DNA on nMOF, the nanoparticles were centrifuged and washed. All the supernatants were collected for calculating the amount of unloaded DNA. The DNA concentrations were determined by UV–Vis spectrometry at \(260\,\mathrm{\nm}\) . Finally, the average number of DNA strands per nMOF was calculated by taking the ratio of loaded DNA amount (in moles) to the particle concentration of nMOF (in moles).
